coder › Done. Here's what I changed:

## Implementation (`src/limiter.ts`)

I implemented `RateLimiter` as a token bucket with two private fields: `tokens` (current balance) and `lastRefill` (timestamp of the last refill). It starts full (`tokens = capacity`).

**`tryTake(n)`**:
- First refills up to the current time.
- Returns `false` and takes nothing if `n > capacity` or `tokens < n`.
- Otherwise deducts `n` and returns `true`.

**`available`**:
- Refills up to the current time and returns the (possibly fractional) balance.

**Refill logic**: a private `refill()` computes elapsed seconds since `lastRefill` via `now()`, adds `elapsed * refillPerSecond`, caps at `capacity`, and only advances `lastRefill` when time moved forward — so a backwards-moving clock just refills nothing rather than corrupting the bucket.

## Tests (`src/limiter.test.ts`)

Added 8 tests on top of the existing "starts full" one (9 total, 36 assertions): taking exact amounts, taking n, refusal when n exceeds capacity, refusal when insufficient (takes nothing), continuous fractional refill over time, a single large time jump refilling to capacity without overshoot, fractional `available` with a partial take, zero-cost zero takes, and a backwards-moving clock.

## Checks

The project's only check is `bun test`:

